zΔ⚡b|=2f1a|+ Variations %3D%3D%0AChanges since 1975 include improvements to the core algorithm, the addition of useful features to the command, and the design of new output formats. The basic algorithm is described in the papers ''An O(ND) Difference Algorithm and its Variations'' by Eugene W. Myers<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DE. Myers%7Ctitle%3DAn O(ND) Difference Algorithm and Its Variations%7Cjournal%3DAlgorithmica%7Cvolume%3D1%7Cissue%3D2%7Cyear%3D1986%7Cpages%3D251–266%7Cdoi%3D10.1007/BF01840446%7Cciteseerx%3D10.1.1.4.6927}}</ref> and in ''A File Comparison Program'' by Webb Miller and Myers.<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor1%3DWebb Miller %7Cauthor2%3DEugene W. Myers %7Ctitle%3DA File Comparison Program%7Cjournal%3DSoftware — Practice and Experience%7Cvolume%3D15%7Cissue%3D11%7Cyear%3D1985%7Cpages%3D1025–1040%7Cdoi%3D10.1002/spe.4380151102%7Cciteseerx%3D10.1.1.189.70 }}</ref> The algorithm was independently discovered and described in ''Algorithms for Approximate String Matching'', by [[Esko Ukkonen]].<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DEsko Ukkonen%7Ctitle%3DAlgorithms for Approximate String Matching%7Cvolume%3D64%7Cjournal%3DInformation and Control%7Cissue%3D1–3%7Cyear%3D1985%7Cpages%3D100–118 %7C doi %3D 10.1016/S0019%2D9958(85)80046%2D2}}</ref> The first editions of the diff program were designed for line comparisons of text files expecting the [[newline]] character to delimit lines. By the 1980s, support for binary files resulted in a shift in the application's design and implementation.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Edit script %3D%3D%3D%0AAn [[Ed (text editor)%7Cedit script]] can still be generated by modern versions of diff with the <code>%2De</code> option. The resulting edit script for this example is as follows:%0A%0A 24a%0A %0A This paragraph contains%0A important new additions%0A to this document.%0A .%0A 17c%0A check this document. On%0A .%0A 11,15d%0A 0a%0A This is an important%0A notice! It should%0A therefore be located at%0A the beginning of this%0A document!%0A %0A .%0A%0AIn order to transform the content of file ''original'' into the content of file ''new'' using {{Mono%7Ced}}, we should append two lines to this diff file, one line containing a <code>w</code> (write) command, and one containing a <code>q</code> (quit) command (e.g. by {{code%7Clang%3Dbash%7Cprintf "w\nq\n" >> mydiff}}). Here we gave the diff file the name ''mydiff'' and the transformation will then happen when we run {{code%7Clang%3Dbash%7Ced %2Ds original < mydiff}}.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Context format %3D%3D%3D%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding the ''context format'' ({{code%7C%2Dc}}) and the ability to recurse on filesystem directory structures ({{code%7C%2Dr}}), adding those features in 2.8 BSD, released in July 1981. The context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped with distributing patches for source code that may have been changed minimally.%0A%0AIn the context format, any changed lines are shown alongside unchanged lines before and after. The inclusion of any number of unchanged lines provides a ''context'' to the patch. The ''context'' consists of lines that have not changed between the two files and serve as a reference to locate the lines' place in a modified file and find the intended location for a change to be applied regardless of whether the line numbers still correspond. The context format introduces greater readability for humans and reliability when applying the patch, and an output which is accepted as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. This intelligent behavior isn't possible with the traditional diff output.%0A%0AThe number of unchanged lines shown above and below a change ''hunk'' can be defined by the user, even zero, but three lines is typically the default. If the context of unchanged lines in a hunk overlap with an adjacent hunk, then diff will avoid duplicating the unchanged lines and merge the hunks into a single hunk.%0A%0AA "{{code%7C!}}" represents a change between lines that correspond in the two files. A "{{code%7C%2B}}" represents the addition of a line, while a blank [[space (punctuation)%7Cspace]] represents an unchanged line. At the beginning of the patch is the file information, including the full path and a [[time stamp]] delimited by a tab character. At the beginning of each hunk are the line numbers that apply for the corresponding change in the files. A number range appearing between sets of three asterisks applies to the original file, while sets of three dashes apply to the new file. The hunk ranges specify the starting and ending line numbers in the respective file.%0A%0AThe command {{code%7Cdiff %2Dc original new}} produces the following output:%0A<source lang%3D"diff">%0A*** /path/to/original%09timestamp%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/new%09timestamp%0A***************%0A*** 1,3 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 1,9 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A%2B This is an important%0A%2B notice! It should%0A%2B therefore be located at%0A%2B the beginning of this%0A%2B document!%0A%2B%0A  This part of the%0A  document has stayed the%0A  same from version to%0A***************%0A*** 8,20 ****%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A%0A%2D This paragraph contains%0A%2D text that is outdated.%0A%2D It will be deleted in the%0A%2D near future.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this dokument. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A%2D%2D%2D 14,21 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this document. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A***************%0A*** 22,24 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 23,29 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  this paragraph needs to%0A  be changed. Things can%0A  be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2B This paragraph contains%0A%2B important new additions%0A%2B to this document.%0A</source>%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Unified format %3D%3D%3D%0AThe ''unified format'' (or ''unidiff'')<ref>{{Cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Detailed%2DUnified.html%7Ctitle%3DDetailed Description of Unified Format%7Clast%3D%7Cfirst%3D%7Cdate%3D%7Cwebsite%3DGNU Diffutils (version 3.7, 7 January 2018)%7Curl%2Dstatus%3Dlive%7Carchive%2Durl%3D%7Carchive%2Ddate%3D%7Caccess%2Ddate%3D}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread%3D164293%7Ctitle%3DUnified Diff Format%7Clast%3Dvan Rossum%7Cfirst%3DGuido%7Cdate%3D%7Cwebsite%3DAll Things Pythonic%7Curl%2Dstatus%3Dlive%7Carchive%2Durl%3D%7Carchive%2Ddate%3D%7Caccess%2Ddate%3D}}</ref> inherits the technical improvements made by the context format, but produces a smaller diff with old and new text presented immediately adjacent. Unified format is usually invoked using the "<code>%2Du</code>" [[command line option]]. This output is often used as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. Many projects specifically request that "diffs" be submitted in the unified format, making unified diff format the most common format for exchange between software developers.%0A%0AUnified context diffs were originally developed by Wayne Davison in August 1990 (in '''unidiff''' which appeared in Volume 14 of comp.sources.misc). [[Richard Stallman]] added unified diff support to the [[GNU%7CGNU Project]]'s diff utility one month later, and the feature debuted in '''GNU diff''' 1.15, released in January 1991. GNU diff has since generalized the context format to allow arbitrary formatting of diffs.%0A%0AThe format starts with the same two%2Dline [[Header (computing)%7Cheader]] as the context format, except that the original file is preceded by "<samp>%2D%2D%2D</samp>" and the new file is preceded by "<samp>%2B%2B%2B</samp>". Following this are one or more '''change hunks''' that contain the line differences in the file. The unchanged, contextual lines are preceded by a space character, addition lines are preceded by a [[plus sign]], and deletion lines are preceded by a [[minus sign]].%0A%0AA hunk begins with '''range information''' and is immediately followed with the line additions, line deletions, and any number of the contextual lines. The range information is surrounded by double%2D[[at sign]]s, and combines onto a single line what appears on two lines in the context format ([[#Context format%7Cabove]]). The format of the range information line is as follows:%0A%0A @@ %2Dl,s %2Bl,s @@ ''optional section heading''<!%2D%2D <source> ruins the italics %2D%2D>%0A%0AThe hunk range information contains two hunk ranges. The range for the hunk of the original file is preceded by a minus symbol, and the range for the new file is preceded by a plus symbol. Each hunk range is of the format ''l,s'' where ''l'' is the starting line number and ''s'' is the number of lines the change hunk applies to for each respective file. In many versions of GNU diff, each range can omit the comma and trailing value ''s'', in which case ''s'' defaults to 1. Note that the only really interesting value is the ''l'' line number of the first range; all the other values can be computed from the diff.%0A%0AThe hunk range for the original should be the sum of all contextual and deletion (including changed) hunk lines. The hunk range for the new file should be a sum of all contextual and addition (including changed) hunk lines. If hunk size information does not correspond with the number of lines in the hunk, then the diff could be considered invalid and be rejected.%0A%0AOptionally, the hunk range can be followed by the heading of the section or function that the hunk is part of. This is mainly useful to make the diff easier to read. When creating a diff with GNU diff, the heading is identified by [[regular expression]] matching.<ref>[https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Sections.html 2.2.3 Showing Which Sections Differences Are in], GNU diffutils manual</ref>%0A%0AIf a line is modified, it is represented as a deletion and addition. Since the hunks of the original and new file appear in the same hunk, such changes would appear adjacent to one another.<ref>[http://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread%3D164293 Unified Diff Format] by [[Guido van Rossum]], June 14, 2006</ref> An occurrence of this in the example below is:%0A%0A<source lang%3D"diff">%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A</source>%0A%0AThe command <code>diff %2Du original new</code> produces the following output:%0A<source lang%3D"diff">%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/original%09timestamp%0A%2B%2B%2B /path/to/new%09timestamp%0A@@ %2D1,3 %2B1,9 @@%0A%2BThis is an important%0A%2Bnotice! It should%0A%2Btherefore be located at%0A%2Bthe beginning of this%0A%2Bdocument!%0A%2B%0A This part of the%0A document has stayed the%0A same from version to%0A@@ %2D8,13 %2B14,8 @@%0A compress the size of the%0A changes.%0A%0A%2DThis paragraph contains%0A%2Dtext that is outdated.%0A%2DIt will be deleted in the%0A%2Dnear future.%0A%2D%0A It is important to spell%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A the other hand, a%0A misspelled word isn't%0A the end of the world.%0A@@ %2D22,3 %2B23,7 @@%0A this paragraph needs to%0A be changed. Things can%0A be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2BThis paragraph contains%0A%2Bimportant new additions%0A%2Bto this document.%0A</source>%0ANote that to successfully separate the file names from the timestamps, the delimiter between them is a tab character. This is invisible on screen and can be lost when diffs are copy/pasted from console/terminal screens.%0A%0AThere are some modifications and extensions to the diff formats that are used and understood by certain programs and in certain contexts. For example, some [[revision control]] systems—such as [[Subversion (software)%7CSubversion]]—specify a version number, "working copy", or any other comment instead of or in addition to a timestamp in the diff's header section.%0A%0ASome tools allow diffs for several different files to be merged into one, using a header for each modified file that may look something like this:%0A%0A Index: path/to/file.cpp%0A%0AThe special case of files that do not end in a newline is not handled. Neither the unidiff utility nor the POSIX diff standard define a way to handle this type of files. (Indeed, such files are not "text" files by strict POSIX definitions.<ref>http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_205 Section 3.206</ref>) The [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program is not aware even of an implementation specific diff output.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Others %3D%3D%3D%0APostprocessors '''sdiff''' and '''diffmk''' render side%2Dby%2Dside diff listings and applied change marks to printed documents, respectively. Both were developed elsewhere in Bell Labs in or before 1981.{{citation needed%7Cdate%3DFebruary 2015}}%0A%0A[[Diff3]] compares one file against two other files. It was originally conceived by Paul Jensen to reconcile changes made by two people editing a common source. It is also used by revision control systems, e.g. [[Revision Control System%7CRCS]],<ref>https://www.gnu.org/software/rcs/manual/merge.html</ref> for [[merge (revision control)%7Cmerging]].%0A%0AGNU diff and diff3 are included in the '''diffutils''' package with other diff and [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] related utilities. [[Emacs]] has [[Ediff]] for showing the changes a patch would provide in a user interface that combines interactive editing and merging capabilities for patch files. Nowadays there is also a '''patchutils''' package that can combine, rearrange, compare and fix context diffs and unified diffs.%0A%0AGNU [[Wdiff]]<ref>https://www.gnu.org/software/wdiff/</ref> is a front end to diff that shows the words or phrases that changed in a text document of written language even in the presence of word%2Dwrapping or different column widths.%0A%0Acolordiff is a Perl wrapper for 'diff' and produces the same output but with pretty 'syntax' highlighting<ref>https://www.colordiff.org/</ref>.%0A%0AUtilities that compare source files by their syntactic structure have been built mostly as research tools for some programming languages;<ref>{{cite journal%7Clast1%3DHorwitz%7Cfirst1%3DSusan%7Ctitle%3DIdentifying the semantic and textual differences between two versions of a program%7Cjournal%3DACM SIGPLAN Notices%7Cdate%3DJune 1990%7Cvolume%3D25%7Cissue%3D6%7Cpages%3D234–245%7Cdoi%3D10.1145/93548.93574%7Curl%3Dhttp://www.cs.wisc.edu/wpis/papers/sigplan90.ps%7Cciteseerx%3D10.1.1.49.3377}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal%7Clast1%3DYang%7Cfirst1%3DWuu%7Ctitle%3DIdentifying syntactic differences between two programs%7Cjournal%3DSoftware: Practice and Experience%7Cdate%3DJuly 1991%7Cvolume%3D21%7Cissue%3D7%7Cpages%3D739–755%7Cdoi%3D10.1002/spe.4380210706%7Cciteseerx%3D10.1.1.13.9377}}</ref><ref>Grass. Cdiff: A syntax directed Diff for C%2B%2B programs. Proceedings USENIX C%2B%2B Conf., pp. 181%2D193, 1992</ref> some are available as commercial tools.<ref>Compare%2B%2B, http://www.coodesoft.com/</ref><ref>SmartDifferencer, http://www.semanticdesigns.com/Products/SmartDifferencer</ref><ref>Cheney, Austin. ''Pretty Diff %2D Documentation''. http://prettydiff.com/documentation.php</ref> Tools exist to compare HTML <ref>''DaisyDiff'', https://code.google.com/p/daisydiff/</ref> and ones for XML have been published by Microsoft and IBM.<ref>''xmldiffpatch'', http://msdn.microsoft.com/en%2Dus/library/aa302294.aspx</ref><ref>''xmldiffmerge'', http://www.alphaworks.ibm.com/tech/xmldiffmerge</ref>%0A%0A'''spiff''' compares [[Computer file%7Cfile]]s' logical not literal differences thus the same as diff but ignoring differences in:%0A# floating point calculations with roundoff errors<ref name%3D"dontcallme">{{cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://github.com/dontcallmedom/spiff%7Ctitle%3Dspiff%7Cauthor%3Ddontcallmedotcom%7Caccessdate%3D2013%2D06%2D16}}</ref><ref>{{cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://stackoverflow.com/a/1489107/2291035%7Cdate%3D2009%2D09%2D28%7Cauthor%3DDavide%7Ctitle%3Dstackoverflow%7Caccessdate%3D2013%2D06%2D16}}</ref><ref name%3D"hpux">{{cite web%7Curl%3Dhttp://hpux.connect.org.uk/hppd/hpux/Text/spiff%2D1.0/%7Ctitle%3DHP%2DUX Porting and Archiving%7Clocation%3DUK%7Cfirst%3DDaniel W%7Clast%3DNachbar%7Cdate%3D1999%2D12%2D01%7Caccessdate%3D2013%2D06%2D13}}</ref>%0A# and [[Space (punctuation)%7Cwhitespace]]<ref name%3D"dontcallme"/><ref name%3D"hpux"/>%0Awhich are generally irrelevant to source code comparison. An extreme inverse is [[cmp (Unix)%7Ccmp]]. [[Telcordia Technologies%7CBellcore]] wrote the original version.<ref name%3D"dontcallme"/><ref name%3D"hpux"/> An [[HPUX]] port is the most current public release. spiff does not support binary files.<ref>{{cite web%7Curl%3Dhttp://www.math.utah.edu/cgi%2Dbin/man2html.cgi?/usr/local/man/man1/spiff.1%7Ctitle%3DSPIFF 1%7Cdate%3D1988%2D02%2D02%7Caccessdate%3D2013%2D06%2D16}}</ref><ref>{{cite web%7Curl%3Dhttp://hpux.connect.org.uk/hppd/hpux/Text/spiff%2D1.0/man.html%7Ctitle%3DMan page%7Clocation%3DUK%7Cfirst%3DDaniel W%7Clast%3DNachbar%7Cdate%3D1988%2D02%2D02%7Caccessdate%3D2013%2D06%2D16}}</ref> spiff outputs to the [[standard output]] in standard diff format and accepts inputs in the [[C (programming language)%7CC]], [[Bourne shell]], [[Fortran]], [[Modula%2D2]] and [[Lisp (programming language)%7CLisp]] [[programming language]]s.%0A%0A%3D%3D|=8c1|
